Ladybird expands each scroll container by 1250px above and below, and
625px to either side. Blink sets the vertical margin from the effective
connection type in LazyLoadMediaObserver::StartMonitoringNearViewport()
and halves it for the horizontal margin. On a 4G connection, Blink uses
1250px vertically. Gecko configures its lazy-loading observer in
DOMIntersectionObserver::CreateLazyLoadObserver(). WebKit uses a 100%
fallback in LazyLoadElementObserver::intersectionObserver().

Chrome 158.0.8127.36 loaded the image 624px beyond a horizontal edge
and left the one at 626px unloaded. It loaded the image 1249px beyond a
vertical edge and left the one at 1251px unloaded. Chrome and Firefox
151.0 both loaded ten of forty images in the 300px carousel before
scrolling. WebKit 26.5 loaded seven.

Ladybird uses the same scroll margin for an animated image when
checking whether its scroller can intersect the observation root.
Chrome and Firefox loaded the image as its transform moved it into
the margin. WebKit loaded it at its starting position. All three
reported the moving target entering a script observer’s scroll margin.

Blink walks the scrollers in IntersectionGeometry::ClipToRoot().
Gecko applies the scroll margin in ComputeTheIntersection(), and WebKit
expands it in IntersectionObserver::computeIntersectionState().

For an explicitly rooted observer, Ladybird stops checking ancestor
clips when it reaches the root. Chrome and Firefox reported a target
moving into the root even when a negative bottom margin would exclude
it from an outer scroller. WebKit reported only the initial state
during the same CSS animation.
